<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  W3C SCXML 6.2 + 6.4: a child session's `<send delay>` is a deadline of the
  machine that invoked it, because the parent is what ticks its children.

  A host that drives a scheduler-owning machine does not poll. It asks the
  engine when it next needs a tick — `time_until_next_scheduled_ms` in Rust,
  `TimeUntilNextScheduled` in Go, `timeUntilNextScheduledMs` in Kotlin,
  `timeUntilNextScheduled` in the C++ AOT engine — sleeps that long, and ticks.
  The parent's tick advances every running child by the same step, so a moment
  a child needs is a moment the host must not step over. An answer that counts
  only the parent's own scheduler is a promise the engine cannot keep: a host
  that walked time by it reached a parent with nothing of its own armed, was
  told there was nothing to wait for, and left a child's timer unfired.

  This parent arms nothing: it only waits for `done.invoke.kid`. The child arms
  two timers of 200 ms one after the other (the second is armed by the first
  firing), so it ends 400 ms after it started. Walking the engine by its own
  answer must therefore reach `finished` in two moves of 200 ms.

    start   the answer is 200, the child's first deadline, not "nothing owed"
    200 ms  the child leaves `first`; the answer is 200 again, for the second
    400 ms  the child reaches `spoke`, and the parent leaves for `finished`
    after   nothing is owed, so the answer is "nothing" again

  Deliberately single-axis: the parent has no `<send>` and no data model
  reads, so the only deadline in the run is the child's. The two timers are
  chained rather than armed together because a host that jumps over the first
  one fires both from the end of the jump, and the second is then dated from
  the wrong instant — which is the same defect seen from the other side.

  The document sits beside its drivers rather than under
  `integration_resources/`: a stem there is a seven-channel contract, and C11
  and the C++ Interpreter expose no next-deadline query to ask. See
  `scripts/regen_a_child_timer_is_a_deadline_of_its_parent.sh`.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml" version="1.0"
       name="a_child_timer_is_a_deadline_of_its_parent" datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="waiting">

  <state id="waiting">
    <invoke type="scxml" id="kid">
      <content>
        <scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml" version="1.0"
               datamodel="ecmascript" initial="first">
          <state id="first">
            <onentry>
              <send event="one" delay="200ms"/>
            </onentry>
            <transition event="one" target="second"/>
          </state>
          <state id="second">
            <onentry>
              <send event="two" delay="200ms"/>
            </onentry>
            <transition event="two" target="spoke"/>
          </state>
          <final id="spoke"/>
        </scxml>
      </content>
    </invoke>
    <transition event="done.invoke.kid" target="finished"/>
  </state>

  <final id="finished"/>
</scxml>
